EP&gt;doc-4840aa86644111d5ef3469f7c7b235ed-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4e57d988d63c9b94876085d3e98172f3-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-5834a32d1d8ba026d1b73b39f15ac3af-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-782d5b7280f59b6dec6235ac536bd41d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-7ab8292a2e846a0ba58fe01d10d30067-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d2">The term "a11y" is an abbreviation for accessibility, a concept that pertains to the design of products, devices, services, or environments for people with disabilities. It encompasses various features and elements aimed at enhancing usability for individuals who may have visual, auditory, physical, speech, cognitive, or neurological disabilities. A critical aspect of accessibility involves the use of screen readers, which ensure that users can navigate and interact with digital content effectively. The term "a11y" is also used to describe accessibility-related elements and is often associated with improving the user experience for people with disabilities by making technology more inclusive and usable. Additionally, "a11y" can refer to an organization dedicated to accessibility, an abbreviation for accessibility features, an accessibility label, and an accessibility-related element or initiative. In the context of web development, "a11y" is a term related to accessibility and usability. The term is closely tied to accessibility features for web elements, which are designed to enhance the user experience for individuals with disabilities. Accessibility features for web elements include attributes and elements that facilitate interaction and navigation for users with disabilities. These features are essential for ensuring that web content is accessible to everyone, regardless of their abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Accessibility features for web elements&lt;SEP&gt;[a11y] is a term related to accessibility features.</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1787067941</data>
      <data key="d6" />
