    <node id="screen_id">
      <data key="d0">screen_id</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity "screen_id" serves as a unique marker for various screen elements and identifiers within a user interface, particularly significant in defining and locating specific sections of a mobile application. It is used to identify and locate different areas such as the home screen and the search results screen. Two distinct screen IDs are mentioned: "search_results" and "home". The term "screen_id" acts as a location identifier for the search results screen, indicating its position within the application's layout. Additionally, it denotes a screen with the ID "home", suggesting another navigational point within the app. These descriptions collectively highlight the multifaceted role of "screen_id" in defining and locating specific sections of a mobile application.

Furthermore, the term "screen_id" is used to denote a screen element with the ID "search_advanced" and another with the ID "category". It is also a location identifier for the search advanced screen. The concept of "screen_id" extends beyond just identifying screens; it is a unique identifier for a screen element in a user interface, serving as an identifier for a specific screen element. Lastly, there is a mention of a screen element with the ID "search_empty", which further emphasizes the diverse roles and uses of "screen_id" within the application's structure.

Additionally, "screen_id" is used to denote a screen element with the ID "subcategory" and another with the ID "category". It is also a location identifier for the cart empty screen. Another description mentions a screen ID with the value "subcategory", a screen ID assigned to a product element in a user interface, a screen with product ID "product", a screen ID with the value "product", a location identifier for a screen element with ID "cart_empty", and a screen ID with the value "cart_empty". These varied usages underscore the versatility of "screen_id" in serving as a unique identifier for different screen elements and locations within a mobile application's interface.&lt;SEP&gt;A screen ID with the value 'subcategory'</data>
