    <edge source="instruction" target="role">
      <data key="d7">294.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The instruction to click refers to performing an action, such as clicking a button, link, or menu item, which is often associated with a specific role or function related to an element. This action can be tied to various elements like a 'Create an Account' link for user registration, a 'Sports &amp; Outdoors' menu item, or a 'Advanced Search' link. The instruction to click is inherently tied to the role it plays in the interaction, whether it's to initiate a command, navigate a menu, or perform a specific task. Examples include clicking the 'Beauty &amp; Personal Care' menu item, the 'Health &amp; Household' menu item, or the 'Tools &amp; Home Improvement' menu item. Additionally, there is a specific instruction to click the 'Advanced Search' link, indicating a direct action to be taken. The instruction to click the 'Tools &amp; Home Improvement' menu item suggests its importance in the context of the interaction. The instruction to click the role itself indicates the action to be performed. The textbox also plays a role in the instruction to click, as it involves the role of the textbox. The instruction to interact with an element further emphasizes the general nature of the action. The instruction to click the textbox 'Price' is related to its role as a textbox, and similarly, the instruction to click the textbox 'Description' is related to its role as a clickable element. The instruction to click the role itself indicates the action to be performed. The instruction to click the menu item 'Home &amp; Kitchen' is related to its role, and the instruction to click the menu item 'Video Games' is related to its role as well. Clicking the menu item 'Grocery &amp; Gourmet Food' is another example of this action. The instruction to click the link and the instruction to click the role itself indicate the action being performed.&lt;SEP&gt;The instruction to click the role.&lt;SEP&gt;The instruction is to click the role</data>
